    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Qwen3L Spike plotted Slickshot Show-Off for {1}{R} instead of casting it directly for {1}{R}. Plotting means it can be cast for free on a later turn, but it costs the same mana now and delays the creature by a full turn. With only 2 Mountains available and the opponent at 17 life while Qwen is at 11 life, tempo is critical. Slickshot Show-Off has flying and haste - casting it directly would allow it to attack this turn (potentially with pump spells and Leyline of Resonance copies for massive damage). By plotting, the flying haste creature sits in exile doing nothing this turn. Additionally, the reasoning confuses Slickshot Show-Off with a different card (claiming it gives other Lizards +1/+1, which it doesn't - it's a Bird Wizard with flying/haste/prowess). The correct play was to cast Slickshot Show-Off directly, then use it as an attacker this combat step, potentially combining with pump spells and Leyline of Resonance triggers for a big damage turn.",
      "action_taken": "Plotted Slickshot Show-Off for {1}{R}, exiling it to cast for free on a later turn, using both Mountains.",
      "better_line": "Cast Slickshot Show-Off directly for {1}{R} to get a flying haste attacker immediately, then attack with it this turn alongside Hired Claw, potentially using pump spells with Leyline of Resonance for massive damage.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 64,
      "player": "Qwen3L Spike",
      "decision_index": 46,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Qwen3L Spike has 2 untapped Mountains and Emberheart Challenger (1R, haste) in hand, but failed to cast it before combat. The reasoning even acknowledges 'I forgot to cast Emberheart Challenger.' Emberheart Challenger is a 2/2 with haste that could have attacked this turn, and as a creature with Prowess, it would benefit from any combat tricks cast. Additionally, attacking with only Hired Claw into an opponent with no untapped blockers means missing 2 damage from the Challenger. The Mountains appear tapped in the snapshot, suggesting mana was spent on something else earlier in the turn (possibly Full Bore from graveyard context), but the player's own reasoning confirms they missed the window to cast it.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Hired Claw without casting Emberheart Challenger beforehand.",
      "better_line": "Cast Emberheart Challenger (1R) before combat using the 2 Mountains, then attack with both Hired Claw and the hasty Emberheart Challenger for significantly more damage. With both Mountains tapped from casting Challenger, combat tricks wouldn't be available, but the extra 2+ damage from Challenger attacking would be worth more.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 66,
      "player": "Qwen3L Spike",
      "decision_index": 47,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Mountain instead of Rockface Village. Mountain produces unrestricted {R} mana needed for burn spells (Lightning Strike, Burst Lightning) to close out the game against an opponent at 7 life. Rockface Village can only produce {R} for creature spells, making it significantly worse when the primary game plan is burning the opponent out.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Mountain",
      "better_line": "Discard Rockface Village instead, preserving the more flexible Mountain to ensure mana for burn spells that can finish the opponent at 7 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 80,
      "player": "KimiK2 ESPN",
      "decision_index": 51,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "At 6 life vs 15, with Leyline of Resonance on board and pump spells in hand (Turn Inside Out, Dreadmaw's Ire), Qwen3L Spike should have cast Emberheart Challenger before combat to attack with two creatures and maximize damage through doubled pump spells. Both enemy creatures are tapped, making this a free attack. With Leyline doubling Turn Inside Out on Emberheart (+6/+0 plus prowess), the damage output would be dramatically higher.",
      "action_taken": "Moved to attackers with only Hired Claw on board, without casting Emberheart Challenger pre-combat.",
      "better_line": "Play Mountain, cast Emberheart Challenger (haste), attack with both Emberheart and Hired Claw. During combat, cast Turn Inside Out on Emberheart (Leyline copies it, Prowess triggers) for massive damage - potentially 9+ power Emberheart plus Hired Claw damage plus Lizard trigger.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 78,
      "player": "Qwen3L Spike",
      "decision_index": 52,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "KimiK2 ESPN has Burst Lightning (2 damage) and Lightning Strike (3 damage) in hand, totaling 5 damage of burn. The opponent is at 6 life. Burnout Bashtronaut has menace (can't be blocked by one creature) and at speed 3+ likely has double strike (max speed is 4, speed is currently at least 3 based on prior context showing it increased to 3 on turn 3, and would have increased further on subsequent turns). With the attack dealing damage plus burn spells, there's almost certainly lethal available. Even without double strike, Bashtronaut (1 power) + Razorkin Needlehead (2 power) = 3 unblocked damage (Emberheart could block Razorkin but not Bashtronaut due to menace). That puts opponent to 3, then Lightning Strike for 3 = lethal. But more importantly, the reasoning says 'Let me shift to precombat main first' suggesting they wanted to cast spells before combat, yet the action taken was attacking with both creatures. The critical issue is that with 3 lands untapped on KimiK2's side, they could have cast Burst Lightning (1 mana) before combat to deal 2 damage (putting opponent to 4), then attack for at least 1-3 damage. Actually, let me reconsider the mana: they have Mountain, Mountain, Riverpyre Verge = 3 mana available. Lightning Strike costs 2 mana, Burst Lightning costs 1 mana = 3 mana total for 5 burn damage. Opponent is at 6 life. 5 burn + any combat damage = lethal. They should cast burn before or after combat to kill the opponent this turn. The attack alone deals at minimum 1 (Bashtronaut unblocked due to menace) + potentially 2 if Razorkin is unblocked = 3 damage, putting opponent to 3. Then Lightning Strike post-combat for lethal. But even if Razorkin is blocked by Emberheart (2/2 vs 2/2, both die due to first strike on Razorkin during KimiK2's turn), Bashtronaut still gets through for 1 (or more with double strike). The real question is whether they followed up with burn spells after combat. The decision itself (attacking with all) seems fine, but the reasoning mentions wanting to go to precombat main first, yet they attacked anyway without casting spells first. If they have lethal with burn after combat, the attack is fine. But if speed is 4 (max), Bashtronaut has double strike dealing 2 damage unblocked, plus Razorkin's 2 = 4 damage minimum, opponent goes to 2, and any burn spell kills. The attack itself isn't the blunder - the potential blunder is not casting burn spells in the right sequence. However, we can only evaluate this specific decision. Attacking with all is correct when you have lethal with burn backup. Actually, re-reading: the opponent has Emberheart Challenger untapped (not tapped) which could block Razorkin Needlehead. So blocks could be: Emberheart blocks Razorkin (can't block Bashtronaut alone due to menace). Result: Bashtronaut gets through. If at max speed (4), double strike = 2 damage, opponent to 4. Razorkin has first strike on KimiK2's turn, deals 2 to Emberheart's 2 toughness, Emberheart dies before dealing damage. Then post-combat: Lightning Strike (3) + Burst Lightning (2) = 5, but only need 4. Lethal confirmed. The attack is correct. But wait - casting Burst Lightning pre-combat targeting a creature (or player) might be better sequencing to avoid potential combat tricks. Actually, the opponent has 2 tapped Mountains so no mana for tricks. The attack is fine. Let me reconsider if this is actually a blunder... The key concern from the reasoning is they said they wanted to go to precombat main but attacked anyway. Since lethal is available regardless of attack order, this isn't a blunder per se.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with both Burnout Bashtronaut and Razorkin Needlehead without casting any spells pre-combat, despite having lethal burn in hand (Burst Lightning + Lightning Strike = 5 damage) with opponent at 6 life",
      "better_line": "Cast Burst Lightning targeting opponent (6→4 life) pre-combat, then attack. This guarantees lethal regardless of blocks since Lightning Strike (3 damage) remains as post-combat finisher, and removes any risk of opponent surviving through unexpected interaction",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 88,
      "player": "KimiK2 ESPN",
      "decision_index": 59,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 2 HP needing to deal 13 damage, targeting Hired Claw with Turn Inside Out instead of Emberheart Challenger missed the Valiant trigger on Emberheart, which would exile the top card of the library and potentially reveal additional burn or pump spells. The Leyline copy could then target the other creature. Emberheart also has prowess giving it +1/+1 from the spell cast, making it a better pump target for maximizing damage.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted Hired Claw with Turn Inside Out",
      "better_line": "Target Emberheart Challenger with Turn Inside Out to trigger Valiant (exile top card, may play it this turn), then use the Leyline copy on Hired Claw or Emberheart again for maximum damage potential",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 93,
      "player": "Qwen3L Spike",
      "decision_index": 70,
    }
